<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1978 CE – 1992 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The communist state of Afghanistan, whose radical reforms and the Soviet invasion of 1979 ignited decades of war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a> seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of April 1978, killing President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Daoud Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan</a> (1973-1978). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> faction became head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Radical land reform, literacy and marriage decrees and the repression of clergy and opponents provoked uprisings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Taraki</a> in 1979, and in December the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded, killing Amin and installing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> faction. A war against the mujahideen, backed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, killed perhaps a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> and drove some five million into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a>, leader from 1986, pursued 'national reconciliation' and renamed the state <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan</a> in 1987.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soviet troops withdrew by February 1989, but the government survived until Soviet aid ended. In April 1992 mujahideen forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-state-of-afghanistan">Islamic State of Afghanistan</a> was proclaimed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan.jpg" alt="Democratic Republic of Afghanistan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Afghanistan_(1978%E2%80%931980).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1978 CE – 1992 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nur Muhammad Taraki, Babrak Karmal, Mohammad Najibullah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (state atheism ideology)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Afghanistan (1987-1992), DRA</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1985&amp;focus=democratic-republic-of-afghanistan" aria-label="Democratic Republic of Afghanistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Democratic Republic of Afghanistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6757 1183M6757 1183L5820 533M6757 1183L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M5820 533h0M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=1985&amp;focus=democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
